1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,300
In 1930, Keynes foresaw a world
rich enough for 15-hour weeks by 2030.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,400
We got the productivity.
Not the time.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:10,300
Since 1979, productivity rose 60%;
typical pay, just 16%.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,900
The gains went mostly to the top.

5
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:15,000
AI is our second chance.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,900
Or the same mistake, faster.

7
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,700
The Sufficiency Ladder: of new AI profit,
90% to workers, 10% to the company.

8
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,500
Near enough?
Work four days, then three.

9
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:27,300
Freed hours go to the next in line.

10
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:30,900
Everyone climbs.
Owners too, just slower.

11
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:35,900
Every piece already exists,
from France to the Netherlands to Alaska.

12
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:37,900
We simulated it.

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,200
Lower-paid incomes grow 4% a year,
savings included, not half a percent.

14
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:45,200
Unemployment: zero, not 17%.
A four-day week.

15
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,900
And no group overtakes another.

16
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:49,900
The key rule:
split profit after costs.

17
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,900
Split before, and most AI
never gets built.

18
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:55,500
Argue with it. Improve it.

19
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:57,000
Paper and simulator on my channel.

20
00:00:57,100 --> 00:00:59,800
Keynes promised us time.
AI is our second chance.
